AB  - Herding and pastoralism are practices that involve a mobile, multi-species collective of humans, herbivores, dogs and vegetation. They developed in the wake of neolithization and have played a major role in shaping many of today's rural geographies. In the face of planetary change, this practice must adapt to the depletion of pastoral resources in drought-prone open environments. It is also at the centre of value conflicts over rural space between its proponents and supporters of rural rewilding, characterised by reforestation and the protection of wild herbivores and predators such as the wolf. This research offers an institutionalist perspective on Mediterranean pastoral geographies, here called saltus. It focuses on conflicts over saltus valorisations and their implications for the reshaping of land tenure relations. It is based on a field study conducted in the Cévennes in southern France, an area inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in 2011 as a paradigmatic example of an evolving and living Mediterranean agro-pastoral cultural landscape. Paradoxically, this inscription has drawn attention to land tenure issues that threaten the viability of pastoral collectives. The abandonment of the countryside, the severe fragmentation of the land, the remoteness and the cultural change of the owners have favoured the individual appropriation of the landscape's amenities to the detriment of the collective practices that have shaped it. However, it has also led to collective action aimed at redefining property relations. This paper presents four land tenure arrangements that are being negotiated by pastoral collectives to maintain or restore saltus land. Led by local actors acting at different scales and promoting different values, these initiatives are generating multiple forms of pastoral land governance. It suggests that saltus areas may be a laboratory where new land tenure arrangements are being invented, based on the primacy of more than human collective use rights over the exclusivity that characterises Western modern private property.
KW  - Values of nature
