TY  - GEN
TI  - The climate/development dilemma in international climate negotiations: History of a climate‐centric reductionism (english traduction) (1990‐2022)
AU  - Cassen, C.
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2025/01/16
LA  - en
AB  - The dissertation examines the persistent climate‐centric approach of the international climate negotiations and its related tension with development issues. It analyzes this tension through the coevolution between the main policy instruments adopted within the UNFCCC and a climate policy engineering, in which techno‐economic modeling occupies a distinctive position. Chapter 1 presents an interdisciplinary approach to analyzing these co‐evolutions and mobilises the concept of path dependency. It draws on a variety of sources, including academic and grey literature, CIRED archives and stakeholder interviews. The second part of the dissertation (chapters 2 and 3) examines two dimensions of a path‐dependent process that has led to a climate‐centric approach, including the coordination of negotiations around targets, from the Kyoto Protocol to the Paris Agreement, and persistent equity concerns about a burden‐sharing approach. The contribution to this path dependent process of energy economic experts from North countries, primarily in international fora and organisations is examined at four key moments: i) the European ecotax project (1990‐1992), ii) the negotiations on the flexibility mechanisms of the Kyoto Protocol (1995‐2001), iii) the adoption of mechanisms dedicated to South countries prior to Bali (NAMAs) (2001‐2007), and iv) the institutionalisation of the 2°C objective (2007‐2014). The third part analyzes the role of Integrated Assessment Models (IAMs) as a specific facet of the climate policy engineering in the climate‐centric approach of the negotiations. Chapter 4 shows that the reorientation of the policy agenda towards energy issues in the 80s had a significant impact on modeling efforts, particularly on the new IAMs that emerged following the institutionalization of the climate change issue. Chapter 5 shows how IAMs then became central in producing scenarios consistent with the 2°C target, based on a 'techno‐centric' approach. To that end, the IAM community relied on a repertoire of modeling practices and close collaboration with IPCC Group III (Chapter 6). The fourth part describes a fifth post‐Paris moment first characterized by a persistent gap between the long‐term transition scenarios generated by IAMs, in line with the IPCC AR5, and the short‐term challenges of accelerating the low‐carbon transition, as discussed in Chapter 7. Chapter 8 also highlights the growing role of modeling communities in the construction of national decarbonisation strategies and the organiszational and scientific challenges they face. The dissertation concludes that the incremental dynamics of change in the UNFCCC policy instruments have co‐evolved with opposite conceptions of the climate‐development nexus within a climate policy engineering, due to both strategies of alignment with the negotiating agenda and internal dynamics (techno‐centric reductionism, North‐South asymmetry, community effect). The conclusion calls for a more in‐depth examination of recent change and continuity within this technoeconomic expertise and of its interfaces within the current climate negotiations.
