TI  - AI-Energy Scenarios in Europe
AU  - Le Goff, Thomas
AU  - Paccou, Rémi
AU  - Wijnhoven, Fons
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2025/11/03
LA  - en
AB  - Artificial intelligence is accelerating demand for electricity in Europe, challenging established approaches to power system analysis and regulation. This paper presents two system dynamics models to quantify potential trajectories for AI-driven electricity consumption and to investigate how regulatory strategies and infrastructure decisions influence grid adequacy and emissions. The first model produces scenario-based projections for total European demand, with outcomes highly sensitive to the speed of infrastructure deployment and regulatory intervention. The second model advances the field by treating regulation itself as a dynamic system variable—its strictness and adaptiveness evolve in response to changing system stress and capacity margins. Results indicate that the interaction between digital demand and grid constraints produces a range of outcomes, from stable growth to adequacy crises, emphasizing the importance of methodologically explicit and endogenous treatment of regulation. This modeling framework delivers a reproducible approach for evaluating risks and pathways for sustainable AI and energy system co-evolution in Europe.
