TI  - AI-Powered Microgrids - The Path Toward Coupling Economic and Environmental Benefits
AU  - Paccou, Rémi
AU  - Kluska, Jacques
AU  - Roussilhe, Gauthier
AU  - Fourboul, Eric
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2025/11/03
LA  - en
AB  - This study examines whether AI-optimized microgrids can simultaneously deliver economic and environmental benefits through a comprehensive net impact assessment of 11 operational sites across Europe, North America, and Australia. Using real-time operational data from 2023-2025, we employ a hybrid attributional-consequential life-cycle assessment framework compliant with ITU-T L.1480 and EGDC methodologies to evaluate both direct digital impacts and indirect systemic effects. Our analysis reveals that basic heuristic microgrids already achieve substantial environmental benefits (−14,100 t CO₂e annually across sites) through enhanced self-consumption and local renewable generation. AI-based optimization delivers strong economic gains (18% cost reduction, €200,000 annual savings) but shows highly context-dependent environmental outcomes. Environmental performance depends critically on the alignment between electricity pricing structures and carbon intensity signals rather than algorithmic sophistication alone. Comparative case analysis demonstrates that where time-of-use tariffs correlate with carbon intensity (e.g., South Australia), AI achieves both cost savings (−€124,500) and emissions reductions (−328.6 t CO₂e). Conversely, in carbon-blind markets (e.g., California), economic optimization (−€27,533) occurs alongside increased emissions (+51.46 t CO₂e). Multi-criteria assessment across 16 environmental indicators confirms that fossil resource depletion and climate impact reductions dominate the environmental profile, with minor trade-offs in mineral depletion and particulate matter. We identify five structural conditions necessary for coupling economic and environmental objectives: (1) carbon-informed electricity tariffs, (2) sufficient local flexibility and renewable capacity, (3) supportive regulatory frameworks, (4) high-resolution carbon intensity data infrastructure, and (5) multi-objective optimization functions. These findings challenge technosolutionist assumptions and demonstrate that AI functions as an amplifier of existing system incentives rather than an inherent decarbonization agent. This work provides one of the first empirical, multi-site assessments quantifying the net environmental and economic impacts of AI-powered energy management systems, offering actionable insights for policymakers, system operators, and technology developers working toward sustainable digital energy transitions. Keywords: artificial intelligence, microgrids, net environmental impact, life-cycle assessment, energy optimization, carbon intensity, distributed energy resources, model predictive control, sustainability, climate mitigation
