TI  - Network analysis reveals phase transitions in agro-food nitrogen systems with contrasting feeding capacity and land requirement
AU  - Fauste-Gay, Adrien
AU  - Unterberger, Jeremie
AU  - Vidal, Olivier
AU  - Mouysset, Lauriane
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026/06/01
LA  - en
AB  - Agro-food transitions are often assessed with optimization models or fixed scenarios that return a single admissible configuration. Such approaches are useful for quantifying performance, but they say little about the diversity of feasible operating modes and the internal organization of nutrient cycling. Here, we investigate whether a phase-based reading of nitrogen metabolism can reveal distinct biophysical regimes of agro-food systems and clarify the conditions under which recycling, autonomy, feeding capacity, and land requirement change together. We develop a continuous-time network framework in which production, allocation, recycling, and losses are represented as mass-conserving flows between compartments. The approach combines stationary resolution with an analytical coarse-graining of dominant cycles to derive decision rules and phase diagrams. We apply it to a stylized nitrogen network parameterized with French reference values and explore three case studies on fertilization, livestock, and diet. We show that agro-food nitrogen systems are organized into distinct metabolic phases separated by sharp transitions in dominant recycling pathways. These phase shifts have direct consequences for agronomic outcomes. In the French reference configuration, cropland-based feeding capacity is about 8.7 people per hectare of cropland, depending on the constraint set, whereas strict autonomy under the current diet requires about 19.7 million hectares of agricultural land. We also show that reducing synthetic fertilization without simultaneous dietary change or redesign of nitrogen sources leads either to strong land expansion or to increased dependence on imports. To our knowledge, this is the first demonstration that a phase-based network analysis can jointly connect nutrient cycling structure, feeding capacity, land requirement, and autonomy in a single analytical framework for agricultural transition assessment.
