AB  - Line ride-sharing (LRS) is a twofold service: the physical fold comprises a road itinerary with access stations for riders to board in drivers' cars and alight from them, together with the digital fold of an information platform to arrange matchings and transfer money. Riders (Users) can arrive at stations without prior notice, hence the dynamic feature. Upon frequent drivers' (agents') runs, the service is a dynamic Auto Rapid Transit mode (dART). To gain theoretical understanding of a dART service, the article provides a supply-demand model at the link level, under steady regime and homogenous demand. The service operator supplies matchings between riders (Users) and drivers (Agents): the "technical laws" yield cab occupation and role wait times as functions of the role flows, depending on the waiting policy either "User Waits" or "Agent Waits". The technical process and a load-related agent reward schedule constitute the economic behavior of the operator. On the demand side, the Agent and User roles make travel options on the link, together with Other modes. By modal option, a utility function is constituted of money and time items by leg type (in-vehicle Running, Waiting, Dwelling and Transaction). Individual mode choice is modeled at two binary nests, respectively Within service (ISS) and between it and Outer options (OSS). At each nest, the probability equation of binary choice combines with the technical laws to yield an economic law: between cab frequency and cab occupation at the inner nest, between service participation rate and service joint utility at the outer nest. The waiting policies yield ( , ) functions on either side of a cut-off cab occupation. Putting the link multimodal trip flow as an Inferred Demand Function of variable , the utility advantage of User over Agent, traffic equilibrium is shown to exist, possibly in degenerate form if service utility is insufficient, which occurs if link flow is below a "critical mass" that stems from the time and money conditions.
