TI  - Metabolic signatures of long-term territorial French Agro-Food Systems with E-GRAFS
AU  - Fauste-Gay, Adrien
AU  - Le Noë, Julia
AU  - Maguet, Mathieu
AU  - Vidal, Olivier
AU  - Mouysset, Lauriane
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026/01/01
LA  - en
DO  - 10.2139/ssrn.6359302
AB  - Understanding agro-food vulnerability requires tracing nitrogen pathways beyond crop livestock aggregates. We test how crop mixes and herd structure shape efficiency and vulnerability in 33 French territories, 1852-2014. We develop E-GRAFS, a physically closed nitrogen accounting that disaggregates crops, livestock, products, population and excreta. Each territory-year is classified into crop livestock bundles and evaluated with NUE plus three network indicators: internal reuse (Finn Cycling Index), route diversity (effective edges), and corridor dependence (global efficiency). Only a small subset of possible bundles recurs; strong specializations emerge mainly after 1980 and remain concentrated. Diversified and meadow-rich systems have higher NUE; pasture and cereal-dominated systems are lower. Vulnerability is multidimensional: arable low-livestock and ultra-specialized bundles show low reuse and few alternatives, while integrated forage-livestock bundles show strong corridor dependence. E-GRAFS makes explicit nitrogen circulation in agricultural systems, providing a process-level reading of territorial metabolisms.
