TI  - Assembling renewable energy resources: materiality, financing and territorialisation
AU  - Nadaï, Alain
AU  - Grouiez, Pascal
AU  - Assié, Arnaud
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2025/04/28
T2  - Finance and Space
SN  - 2833-115X
VL  - 2
IS  - 1
LA  - en
DO  - 10.1080/2833115X.2025.2489415
AB  - This paper explores the relationship between the processes through which renewable energy (RE) resources are developed, the types of financing used for RE projects, and the relations these projects establish with local actors (territorialisation). Our analysis draws on anthropological, science and technology studies (STS) and geography approaches to resource materiality, which we conceptualise as an assemblage of substances, technologies, discourses and practices used by actors. Comparing case studies in wind power, solar photovoltaic (PV) and biogas in France, we identify three modes of assembling the resource: stabilising a recipe (biogas), empowering developers (wind power) and modularising the flow-energy conversion (solar PV). We discuss how each mode supports a particular form of territorialisation – that is, a distribution of materiality and financing among local actors. This framing helps analyse the relationship between materiality, financing and territorialisation. The paper shows that, in each case, these assemblages give the conversion of flows (wind, solar radiation) or materials (slurry, energy crops) into energy a specific capacity to integrate into social relations and spatial contexts. This, in turn, shapes how energy production is distributed within society and influences the potential of renewable energy projects to engage local actors in their financing and development – in other words, to become territorialised.
SP  - 95
EP  - 115
