LA  - en
AB  - At a time of planetary boundaries and resource scarcity, the prioritisation of needs and green planning are increasingly part of public debate. The combination of these two concerns has historical precedents. This paper examines the case of British scientific humanism, a movement that developed in the 1930s and 1940s and that gathered natural scientists (biologists, physiologists) interested in social and economic affairs. These scientists made the distinction between scientifically assessed biological and social needs of human beings and the postulation of subjective economic preferences (or desires), giving priority to the satisfaction of the former. They also paid attention to democratic planning as a means to satisfy those needs efficiently without lapsing into technocracy or jeopardising individual freedoms. In addition to enriching the historiography, this paper shows that sources of inspiration do exist to help us design planning strategies respectful of democratic values.
