AB  - In January 2024, the French Association for the Development of Research in Economics and Statistics (ADRES), a research group of CNRS Social Sciences &amp; Humanities, organized a roundtable on the prospects for the economics discipline in its consideration of environmental issues. This article reports on the main outcomes of the roundtable, based on the specific views of the speakers. It shows the main theoretical and methodological challenges facing researchers, in terms of data collection, measurements, and modelling. It also points out some stringent topics to be addressed in the near future: risks and uncertainties, sufficiency and post-growth, social justice, animal welfare, adaptation of production systems. It finally emphasizes the challenges of conducting economic research on the environment. Three conclusions can be derived: (1) normative transparency is important to gain accuracy and confidence from stakeholders; (2) interdisciplinary research is key in environmental studies; and (3) the environment poses major and profound challenges to the entire profession, not just to environmental economists.
