TY  - THES
TI  - Balancing materials needs with carbon footprint mitigation: an integrated climate-economy-materials prospective for France
AU  - Teixeira, Antoine
PY  - 2024
DA  - 2024/09/26
LA  - en
AB  - Global Greenhouse Gas (GHG) emissions related to the production of base materials are experiencing rapid growth, driven by global industrial and economic development. These emissions account for nearly one quarter of global emissions and pose major challenges in the fight against climate change. In particular, existing National Low-Carbon Strategies (NLCS) only focus on reducing territorial emissions and are incompatible with the globalized and carbon-intensive nature of materials supply chains. In that context, there is a pressing need to identify national strategies that can balance materials needs with carbon footprint reduction. This thesis explores various aspects of this question by developing integrated modeling experiments for France. The first part of this thesis examines the role of materials needs in shaping current and future carbon footprints. Through a Multi-Regional Input-Output (MRIO) analysis, an introductory chapter highlights the North-South disparity regarding the Carbon Footprint of Materials (CFM) today. It demonstrates that direct GHG emissions from materials production are predominantly concentrated in the Global South, driven by the Global North's final consumption. The absence of suitable tools to assess and map the CFM at a country scale for present situation and future scenarios motivated the development of the Hybrid Input-Output (HIO) MatMat model. Detailed in the second chapter, this tool supports all the applications developed for the case of France of the thesis. The third chapter demonstrates that French NLCS does not cover the mitigation of its CFM. In 2015, it amounted to about 3 tCO2eq per capita, with 90% of it embodied in imports. By 2050, it is projected not to decrease under the latest official NLCS. Mapping the CFM along value chains and international trade reveals potential strategies to mitigate these emissions. The second part of the thesis evaluates two alternative strategies to balance materials needs with carbon footprint reduction in France: energy sufficiency and materials recycling. The fourth chapter shows that adopting greater energy sufficiency can more effectively reduce France’s carbon and materials footprints in 2050, thus enhancing sustainability of consumption and production patterns. The fifth chapter explores materials recycling as a mitigation lever, demonstrating that prioritizing recycling to reduce imports rather than the production of primary materials is more effective in decreasing carbon and material footprints. This approach also yields to higher socio-economic co-benefits, such as job creation and reduction in trade deficits, while enhancing resilience against price volatility in international markets. Additionally, this chapter introduces an innovative modeling framework by combining the MatMat model with the Computable General Equilibrium (CGE) model Imaclim-S France, thereby highlighting the added value of CGE models over traditional IO analyses in assessing induced economic impacts. To conclude, this thesis underscores the need to improve and expand existing tools, currently limited to integrated energy-material analyses in the field of industrial ecology, to better represent key economic mechanisms that significantly influence prospective results. It calls for a rethinking of public policies and national low-carbon strategies to integrate the carbon footprint and broader environmental impacts. Notably, it proposes enhancing the integration of circular economy practices in transition scenarios, moving beyond recycling as a last-resort strategy in a robust sustainability framework.
