TY  - THES
TI  - Climate Policy and the Low-Carbon Transition: Overcoming Investment Barriers and Managing Macro-Financial Risks
AU  - Sarre, Angélique
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2025/12/11
VL  - 63
LA  - en
AB  - Addressing climate change demands massive investment, yet redirecting financial flows toward low-carbon technologies faces significant obstacles. Among these, high risks associated with low-carbon investments —stemming from innovation uncertainties, long payback periods, and substantial upfront costs— deter investors and raise financing costs. Understanding the implications of these risks is therefore essential for designing effective climate policies. This dissertation examines how the presence of high investment risks affects the effectiveness of climate policies and assesses their potential economic and financial implications. Chapter 1 evaluates the impact of four climate policies on the economy, the financial system, and the low-carbon transition under two scenarios of risk on green investment: (i) a carbon tax; (ii) a public guarantee on green loans; (iii) a mandate, and (iv) green public procurement. To this end, we develop a stock-flow consistent model that accounts for path dependency, investment risks, and financing costs. Simulations reveal that high investment risks, and therefore, high financing costs, weaken the effectiveness of climate policies while amplifying transition risks, negatively affecting the economy. These findings underscore that no single policy instrument is universally effective. Their performance is highly sensitive to investment risk conditions. At the same time, the instruments exhibit complementary effects, suggesting that deploying a combination of instruments is more cost-effective than relying on one type of instrument. Chapter 2 delves deeper into the analysis of green public guarantees by examining discrepancies between actual risk and perceived risk on green investment. Chapter 1 showed that guarantees alone are insufficient to drive the transition when green investments are highly risky, as the reduction in financing costs they provide may not be enough to accelerate firms’ green investments. Nonetheless, there is growing interest in using concessional public guarantees to support the low-carbon transition. Building on this, Chapter 2 evaluates the impacts of a green public guarantee across different risk scenarios, considering: actual risks reflecting default probabilities; firms’ perceived investment risks; banks’ perceived risks affecting green financing costs. Results show that guarantees are most effective when high financing costs arise from overestimation of risks by banks due to limited familiarity with green investments. Even in this context, the efficiency of the guarantee program depends on the investment grade of the guarantor. When actual risks are high, additional de-risking instruments are necessary to reduce default rates. This chapter reveals that guarantees are not only sensitive to investment risks but also to the perception of these risks. Chapter 3 builds on Chapters 1 and 2, suggesting that the financial system plays a key role in the transition, influencing not only its speed but also its financial and economic impacts. However, most integrated assessment models (IAMs), used to assess the economic impacts of the low-carbon transition, do not explicitly consider the financial sector. In this context, Chapter 3 reviews the main motivations for modelling finance and introduces an explicit representation of the financial sector into an integrated energy-economy framework. Including feedback loops between the financial sector and the broader economy produces significantly different macroeconomic outcomes than models without these interactions. These results highlight the need to represent the financial sector in more detail in IAMs to better capture the macroeconomic impacts of the low-carbon transition.
