TY  - THES
TI  - Mobility as a Service (MaaS) as concept and reality : a forward-thinking exploration considering forms of cooperation
AU  - Daou, Sylvain
PY  - 2025
DA  - 2025/12/18
LA  - en
DO  - 10.70675/e92f43aczc0a9z4390za256zc30fdfed773a
AB  - In a sociotechnical context marked by the rapid development of digital services and the multiplication of platform-based mobility solutions, Mobility as a Service (MaaS) has emerged as a promising concept vowing to bridge the gap between mobility services in order to enable seamless, door-to-door multimodal trips. Advocates of MaaS have been forecasting major benefits for mobility systems, such as improved accessibility, reduced dependence on private cars, and lower greenhouse gas emissions.Yet, more than ten years after the emergence of the concept and the proliferation of MaaS implementations, significant uncertainties remain regarding its capacity to deliver on this vision of seamless and decarbonized mobility. These uncertainties concern both the tangible outcomes (MaaS uptake levels, the extent and nature of modal shifts, etc.) and the viability of MaaS’ business model (economic profitability, value creation, etc.).In turn, this thesis pursues two main goals. The first, theoretical one is to understand MaaS through an analysis of its conceptualization, its implementations, and its potentiality. The second, more practical one is to develop a systematic approach for staging possible trajectories for the development of MaaS in a given territory, and to apply the proposed approach to a specifically designed case study.To achieve these objectives, this research is organized in two parts. In the first part, after exploring MaaS as a theoretical concept by tracing the evolution of its definitions and reviewing MaaS demand and supply models in the academic literature, MaaS is analyzed in terms of practical implementation, through an overview of 31 MaaS initiatives around the world. This is followed by a critical redefinition of MaaS, considering additional opportunities for cooperation among mobility services. In the second part, we envision MaaS in an urban territory by developing a case study of the Lyon Metropolitan Area. This part begins with a territorial diagnostic in terms of population, people mobility and the transport system, with special focus on mobility services, considered in terms of actors’ resources. This is followed by a scenario-building endeavor to identify plausible alternative futures of MaaS development. Finally, a focused case study of interactions and potential synergies between two services (line ride-sharing and public transit) on a selected road corridor is conducted on the basis of traffic modeling and impact evaluation.
