TY  - THES
TI  - Mobility footprints per unit length and per lived day : contributions to the holistic assessment of transport modes and individual usage
AU  - Ruales Calambas, Edwin
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026/01/26
LA  - en
AB  - Individuals carry out their daily mobility using transport modes whose use and manufacture generate social, economic, and environmental impacts. Beyond individual concerns, such as time spent, fatigue, or money spent, the consumption of collective resources, including public space for circulation and public funds, as well as the emissions of noise, air pollutants, and greenhouse gases (GHG), represent collective stakes.This thesis proposes a multidimensional assessment of mobility impacts, structured as a system of footprints designed to reflect the different societal stakes involved. Four categories are addressed: (i) user conditions, in terms of time spent and monetary expenditure; (ii) environmental pressures through local pollutant and GHG emissions; (iii) the time-area occupation of public road space; and (iv) the macroeconomic impacts of final mobility expenditures, including both household spending and public revenue transfers.For the latter two categories, the thesis provides methodological contributions. Regarding the Time-Area Footprint of road space occupation, several vehicle circulation regimes are distinguished, and equivalences are established between the occupancy indicators respectively used by traffic engineers and urban planners. For the Macroeconomic Footprint, the analysis traces the value flows generated by final expenditures related to user payments and public subsidies, using the intersectoral input-output matrix of intermediate consumption and social transfers, up to the net impacts by macroeconomic agent category, including public administrations and social accounts.The footprint system is expressed using two functional units: the passenger-kilometre, as a unit of space traversed by an individual, and the lived day, as a unit of “human time”.To operationalise the evaluation methodology, a mobility microsimulator was developed. It enumerates individuals and daily chains of activities and trips, sampling the population of a given territory and its mobility performance. Impacts are computed by category based on locally parameterised unit factors. An application is conducted for the Île-de-France region, describing the inhabitants’ mobility patterns using data from the 2018 Household Travel Survey. The results show that modal performance cannot be judged on a single dimension. The car, although individually efficient and a net contributor to public finances, becomes spatially inefficient in dense areas. Public transport is highly structuring in dense zones but strongly dependent on public subsidies. Active modes, finally, display the smallest footprints but within limited spatial ranges over the course of a lived day.Two typological classifications were established: one for individuals based on their demographic, socio-economic, and spatial attributes, and another for lived days, described as daily chains of activities and trips that distinguish transport modes and integrate multiple impacts. The twelve individual profiles and twelve types of lived days reveal the contrasts between constrained and chosen mobility, between car dependency and urban multimodality. In particular, the “daily journey” and the “travel time budget” differentiate types of days: the longest journeys rely on motorised modes, and the automobile remains the mode that allows time budgets to be contained.Overall, this research is systemic and interdisciplinary, at the crossroads of traffic engineering, impact assessment, urban planning, and economics. It considers the mobility system as a collective organisation of shared resources.
