TY  - THES
TI  - Biodiversity-related investment : the contextual adequacy of financing mechanisms
AU  - Gonon, Morgane
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026/10/05
LA  - en
AB  - In 2022, 188 governments adopted a global biodiversity framework, committing to redirecting USD 500 billion/year of harmful public subsidies (Target 18) and to increasing financial resources dedicated to biodiversity by USD 200 billion/year (Target 19) through "adequate means" (Goal D). This dissertation proposes to define the adequacy of a financing mechanism by its capacity to encode the ecological, economic, and institutional properties of the context in which it is deployed. Contextual adequacy is a condition of a mechanism's effectiveness, and assessing it helps identify the ecological priorities it can support.The dissertation first introduces a heuristic framework mapping funding sources, implementing agents, and biodiversity actions within a given economic context. Then, across four chapters, it demonstrates that a disaggregated and dynamic view of financing needs is necessary for designing adequate mechanisms and monitoring them.The methodological proposal consists of four steps: selecting real-world case studies, mapping the heterogeneity of agents, actions, and funding sources, assessing them against needs or targets, and formally expressing adequacy. This multi-scale and multi-country approach aims to account for the distinct scopes and effects of each mechanism.Chapter 1 focuses on international biodiversity aid to Low-Income Countries and Middle-Income Countries. It classifies 5.4 million projects funded between 2000 and 2023. While aid grew from USD 2.8 to 15.1 billion, it remained fragmented. Three archetypes are stable over time: pollution abatement infrastructure funded by multilateral loans, sustainable agriculture in low-income countries, and forest governance funded by bilateral grants. 65% of the identified projects do not carry a Rio marker.Chapter 2 examines land restoration in Senegal, a low-income country. Projects funded by voluntary carbon credits between 2007 and 2023 exhibit significantly lower ecological and organisational complexity than other restoration projects. A proposed interpretation based on transaction cost factors aims to explain the distribution of funding, opening up avenues for coordination between mechanisms.Chapter 3 addresses the public financing of economic pathways that are less harmful to biodiversity in France, a high-income country. Using a multidimensional, intra-sectoral scoring system, it identifies €27 billion in harmful subsidies in 2022. One-quarter of the subsidies allocated to emission reduction induce land-use change. Among the drivers of biodiversity loss, pollution receives the highest amount of subsidies.Chapter 4 examines the reduction of fiscal and macroeconomic vulnerabilities associated with Colombia's ecological transition, a middle-income country. It simulates four green debt mechanisms within a Stock-Flow Consistent macroeconomic model coupled with multi-objective optimisation and robustness analysis. Principal reduction covers up to 38% of green investment needs and appears in over 80% of Pareto-optimal strategies, though no single lever is sufficient on its own. Robust strategies rely heavily on local-currency bonds.The results cannot be generalised beyond the cases studied, but allow for the extraction of cross-cutting adequacy criteria to rethink ecological conditionality in finance. Adequacy is both temporal and temporary; a mechanism is adequate when its scope of action aligns with pre-identified ecological needs, when it is embedded in a policy mix, and when it mobilises actors who possess the capacity to implement it. Under these conditions, financing mechanisms can contribute to biodiversity conservation and to transformative changes within value chains.
